## 7. Local Installation & Execution Guide

**Prerequisites:** Python 3.9+, Node.js 16.0+ with npm.

### Backend & ML

```bash
# 1. Clone the repository
git clone https://github.com/EarlBatac/<your-repo-name>.git
cd <your-repo-name>

# 2. Create and activate a virtual environment
python -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate          # macOS / Linux
# venv\Scripts\activate           # Windows

# 3. Install dependencies
pip install flask flask-cors pandas numpy xgboost scikit-learn matplotlib

# 4. Run the ETL pipeline (generates Federated_Electronics_Data.csv)
python preprocess.py

# 5. (Optional) Run offline training & validation
#    Generates Training_Progress.png and Feature_Importance.png
python model_training.py

# 6. Start the Flask API on http://127.0.0.1:5000
python app.py
```

### Frontend

In a second terminal, from the React project directory:

```bash
npm install
npm start        # serves the SPA at http://localhost:3000
```

> Keep the Flask server running while using the dashboard; the frontend calls `http://127.0.0.1:5000`.

---

## 8. Limitations & Future Roadmap

### Current limitations
- **Static data:** relies on historical open-source CSV files rather than a live cloud database stream.
- **Limited external signals:** no competitor pricing or unstructured market signals.
- **Seasonal node variance:** accuracy can fluctuate when historical event samples are sparse.

### Future roadmap
- [ ] **LSTM deep learning** — originally proposed to capture complex repetitive sequential patterns alongside XGBoost; not implemented in the current version.
- [ ] **Live ERP integration** — expand API integrations for live database ingestion.
- [ ] **MLOps** — continuous model training pipelines.

